  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 AM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nUpper-level ridging, along with strong high pressure to the\neast, will keep conditions warm and dry through the workweek\nwith highs rising to near 80 later in the week and lows rising\nto the 50s. Patchy morning fog continues to be a daily issue.\nWhile we have had an extended period of dry weather, winds will\ncontinue to be light, limiting fire weather potential.\n\nThe primary consideration remains the arrival of rains from\nwhat is currently Tropical Storm Vance off the western coast of\nMexico. Bringing plenty of moisture, albeit more so at elevated\nlevels, this system is likely to affect the Quad State over the\nweekend, though timing varies. PoPs max out around 50-60%\nSaturday evening through early Sunday, with northeastern areas\nmore likely to have rain hold off until after most Halloween\nactivities are complete, with SEMO most likely to be affected.\nThe blend did put thunder back in for southernmost portions of\nSEMO Saturday afternoon-night, but instability remains limited\nand we remain on the fringes of thunderstorm activity. QPF \nremains fairly low, order of a quarter to half inch Saturday \nevening through Sunday. The high moisture levels from the \nremnants of a tropical system can exceed that, though would have\nto do so by a lot to produce flooding issues.\n\nWhile the tropical remnants move through over the weekend, an\nupper low moves into the Pacific northwest while moisture\narrives at the base of a deep trough in Southern California.\nOperational global models favor taking these two features \nthrough the Rockies and producing a broad, developing system \nover the Central Plains. Of course, being roughly a week out and\ninvolving elements from multiple systems, timing varies across \nmodels, along with positioning, so PoPs are limited and spread \nout for now. Temperatures will remain above normal through early\nnext week.",
